ipping the spinach from the bowl directly into the pan**. The bowl is being used to transfer the spinach into the cooking vessel. The action is deliberate and controlled, suggesting a step in the recipe rather than a casual dump.\n\n---\n\n**4) What happens to the contents of the glass bowl?**\n\nThe contents — the **4 cups of fresh spinach** — are **added to the pan** that already contains a mixture of cooked ingredients (likely ground meat, beans, and tomatoes). In **Frame 2**, the spinach is being poured in. In **Frame 3**, the cook uses a wooden spoon to **stir and mix the spinach into the hot filling**, causing it to begin wilting. By **Frame 4**, the spinach has **completely wilted and is fully incorporated** into the mixture, blending in color and texture with the other ingredients.\n\n---\n\n**5) Is oil being heated in the pot?**\n\nWhile **no visible pool of oil is shown** in the pan in any of the frames, the **presence of oil is strongly implied**. The ingredients in the pan (ground meat, beans, tomatoes) appear to be sautéed and are glistening slightly, which suggests they were cooked in oil or fat. Additionally, the spinach is wilting quickly upon contact with the pan’s contents, indicating the pan is hot — consistent with oil being heated prior to adding the other ingredients. Although not directly visible, **oil was almost certainly heated in the pan earlier in the cooking process** to sauté the base ingredients before the spinach was added.\n\n---\n\n**Summary of Observations:**\n\n- The pan is a copper-finish non-stick skillet, not blue iron.\n- The glass bowl holds fresh spinach, not marinated meat.\n- The bowl is used to pour spinach into the pan.\n- The spinach is stirred in and wilts into the mixture.\n- Oil is not visible but was likely used to heat and cook the ingredients prior to adding spinach.\n\nThese frames capture the step of adding and wilting spinach into a pre-cooked savory mixture — a common technique in healthy, one-pan meal prepara